data: {"delta":{"partial_json":"{\"file_path\": \"/tmp/m1_summary.md\", \"content\": \"# Mission m1 summary: greet, tested\\n\\n## Outcome\\nAll three stated criteria are satisfied:\\n\\n1. `src/greet.py` defines `greet(name)` returning `f\\\"Hello, {name}!\\\"`.\\n2. `tests/test_greet.py` covers a normal name (`greet(\\\"World\\\")`) and the\\n   empty string (`greet(\\\"\\\")`).\\n3. `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` exits 0 (\\\"Ran 2 tests ... OK\\\").\\n\\n## How it was done\\n- Dispatched the work to `dev` (dev/d002) with the exact mission requirements.\\n- `dev` implemented both files and committed to its own branch\\n  (`cs-sandbox/<DEV:1>.<GROUP:1>`, commit `b03ae99`,\\n  \\\"Add greet(name) function with unittest coverage\\\"), reporting a clean\\n  local test run.\\n\\n## Independent verification (not just trusting the report)\\n- Fetched dev's branch (`cs-campaign-member fetch dev`); confirmed\\n  `refs/remotes/campaign/dev/subject` shows the tree differs from base\\n  with real changes.\\n- Read the actual blobs of `src/greet.py` and `tests/test_greet.py` from\\n  that commit to confirm content matches the requirement exactly.\\n- Checked out that commit into an isolated detached git worktree\\n  (`/tmp/verify_greet`) and re-ran `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests`\\n  myself: `Ran 2 tests in 0.000s / OK`, exit status 0.\\n- Accepted dev/d002 only after this independent verification passed.\\n\\n## Orchestrator branch\\nAs orchestrator I wrote no code; my own branch\\n(`cs-sandbox/<ORCHESTRATOR:1>.<GROUP:1>`) carries no source\\nchanges, only campaign notes (plan and assessment) — that is expected for\\nthis role and this mission.\\n\\nEvery stated criterion is met; nothing remains unmet.\\n\"}","type":"input_json_delta"},"index":1,"type":"content_block_delta"}

